21:45:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Religious ideology is certainly a major part of the explanation. The Islamic Republic defines itself through principles that constrain what many leaders can publicly compromise on: resistance to U.S. influence, opposition to Israel, support for allied Islamist movements, and suspicion of Western political and cultural influence. Those commitments can make economically sensible or diplomatically available options politically unacceptable. Critics—including many Iranians—argue that this is precisely the problem: when ideology is treated as non-negotiable, leaders can sacrifice trade, investment, diplomatic relations, and living standards to preserve ideological purity and revolutionary credentials. But the institutional factors are not merely decoration. They help explain *why* ideological priorities continue even when they are costly. Powerful organizations and constituencies benefit from the system: security bodies, parts of the Revolutionary Guard, patronage networks, hardline clerical and political factions, and economic actors tied to sanctions evasion or state control. Ideology supplies legitimacy and a common language; institutions supply power and material interests. And Iranian leaders have sometimes acted pragmatically when they judged it necessary: accepting a ceasefire with Iraq in 1988, negotiating the 2015 nuclear agreement, cooperating at times with neighboring governments, and maintaining significant commercial ties with countries whose politics they do not share. That suggests ideology does not override every calculation. Rather, it narrows the range of acceptable choices and can push decision-makers toward confrontational policies even when the economic costs are obvious. So it is fair to say that ideological rigidity has often produced poor outcomes. The added institutional analysis is not meant to soften that judgment; it explains why the pattern can persist despite those outcomes.
