21:46:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right that this is not just your personal view. A number of Middle East analysts, Iranian dissidents, journalists, economists, and former officials argue that the Islamic Republic’s leadership repeatedly makes self-defeating choices because ideological rigidity, distrust, and refusal to compromise override opportunities to reduce isolation and improve living conditions. That critique often points to recurring patterns: - Treating compromise with outside powers as weakness or betrayal. - Escalating regional confrontations that generate sanctions or military risk. - Rejecting or undermining domestic reforms that could improve legitimacy and economic resilience. - Assuming external pressure proves hostility, then using that hostility to justify even more confrontational policies. - Preserving the political position of hardline institutions even when doing so damages national economic interests. “Stubbornness” can be a useful plain-language description of that pattern. In analytic terms, writers may call it ideological rigidity, threat perception, path dependence, elite-interest protection, or an authoritarian system’s inability to correct errors. Those are not necessarily competing explanations; they can describe different parts of the same dynamic. Still, one article or analyst does not settle the question. There is also substantial scholarship arguing that Iran’s behavior is shaped by real security concerns and that some apparently self-defeating actions have deterrent or domestic-regime logic. The strongest balanced conclusion is that Iranian policy can be strategically motivated while also being persistently damaging, overly rigid, and poorly responsive to its costs.
